-- title:
--   Points of a p-divisible group are integral, Galois-equivariantly
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring, $p$ a prime, $h$ a natural number, and let $G$ be a $p$-divisible group of height $h$ over $R$ in the sense of the project's structure: a family of commutative $R$-Hopf algebras $G_v$ ($v\in\mathbb N$) that are cocommutative, finite and free of rank $p^{vh}$ over $R$, together with surjective coalgebra $R$-algebra maps $G_{v+1}\to G_v$ whose kernels are the ideals generated by the image of the augmentation ideal under multiplication by $p^v$. Let $L$ be any commutative $R$-algebra and $\mathcal O=$ `integralClosure R L` the $R$-subalgebra of elements of $L$ integral over $R$; for an $R$-algebra $S$, $G(S)$ denotes the direct limit over $v$ of the groups of $R$-algebra maps $G_v\to S$ under convolution, written additively, and $T_p(G(S))$ the $\mathbb Z_p$-module of sequences $(x_n)$ in $G(S)$ with $p^nx_n=0$ and $px_{n+1}=x_n$, with the natural action of $R$-algebra automorphisms of $S$. The conclusion is fourfold: (i) the map $G(\mathcal O)\to G(L)$ induced by the inclusion is bijective; (ii) every $\sigma\in\mathrm{Aut}_R(L)$ admits $\sigma'\in\mathrm{Aut}_R(\mathcal O)$ with $\sigma'x=\sigma x$ in $L$ for all $x\in\mathcal O$; (iii) for any such pair $(\sigma,\sigma')$ the map of (i) intertwines the action of $\sigma'$ on $G(\mathcal O)$ with that of $\sigma$ on $G(L)$; and (iv) there is a $\mathbb Z_p$-linear isomorphism $e:T_p(G(\mathcal O))\to T_p(G(L))$ acting componentwise by the map of (i) and satisfying $e\circ\sigma'=\sigma\circ e$ on Tate modules for every such pair $(\sigma,\sigma')$.
--
--   This is the standard observation that points of a $p$-divisible group with values in an algebra automatically take values in the integral closure of the base, so that the Tate module may be computed with integral points without changing the Galois action. It is used in the treatment of Cartier duality and of the pairing between the Tate module and $p$-adic completions, notably by the results on $\mathbb Z_p$-valued pairings over rings of integers.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_PDivisibleGroup_bijective_pointsMap_val_integralClosure_and_exists_tateModule_equiv.lean

